{"agent_id":"pp3g-GpoH-you-are-one-of-several-independent-adversarial-fc0a","agent_seq":7,"timestamp":"2026-08-25T03:51:40.194324928Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"c429e3d9-1c9c-484b-b5a4-fe76ed32c7a4","comment_uuid":"3e8fd3a7-dbf5-4423-8ebe-f7d34bb215fb","author":"pp3g-GpoH-you-are-one-of-several-independent-adversarial-fc0a","content":"VERDICT: CONDITIONAL — Observer v1 is architecturally sound and the F1–F6 feature set is genuinely implemented (not stubbed), but I found 7 MAJOR gaps concentrated in exactly the audited lenses: kill-authority guards, backup data integrity, crash idempotency, and instance safety. None are CRITICAL (no proven data-destroying bug in the default config), but M1/M2/M3 each recreate a loss class this project has already bled on (#460 recovery). Reviewer: pp3g-GpoH, independent adversarial review per #466. Line refs = main HEAD 827204cb.\n\nREVIEW METHOD: read observer.sh (2489 ln) + run-tests.sh (528 ln) in full; verified against .design/lifecycle-manager-design.md, the #460 consolidated dispatch spec (2026-08-24 plan comments), and .crosslink/knowledge/data-retention.md; cross-checked agent-liveness.py verdict matrix (all six verdicts handled — no orphan class); ran bash -n (OK) and executed the harness: 96/98 from a /tmp extraction — both fails are T16 issue-exists/issue-claimed, root-caused to CWD-hermeticity (crosslink CLI needs repo context; see F20), consistent with builder's 98/98 in-repo.\n\n===================== MAJOR =====================\n\n[F1] MAJOR — Parked-expiry auto-kill has NO re-verification, and resume_at can be stale or misparsed with no sanity floor. observer.sh:1237-1243: at resume_at expiry act_frozen fires unconditionally — unlike the LIKELY-FROZEN/STALE-SUSPECT paths which re-check rate_limited first (:1292-1294, :1302-1304), the expiry path never confirms the rate-limit signature is gone, never requires multi-cycle confirmation, and ignores commit freshness. Feeding it: parse_retry_after (:587-615) accepts a bare-seconds form (:600-604, 'retry-after: 30' → now+30s → kill one cycle later even if quota needs hours), assumes UTC for 'daily reset at HH:MM' (:605-614), and act_parked (:1159-1179) happily stores an already-expired timestamp parsed from OLD tail lines — a re-park from stale tail evidence makes expiry immediate. Net effect: a live agent correctly waiting on quota can be auto-killed because of a misparse. This is the weakest-evidence trigger of the kill authority.\nWHY-NOT-CRITICAL: default window 60min and bounded parse formats make premature kills an edge, not a certainty; but the asymmetry vs the guarded paths is real.\n\n[F2] MAJOR — Off-server backup integrity is claimed but not delivered: rclone sync failure does NOT hold watermarks, has NO retry, and export artefacts are never remote-read-back-verified. Code: watermark advances immediately on local GREEN (:2046-2069); rclone_sync_and_verify runs AFTER (:2080-2092) and only rclone-cat-verifies the INDEX files, never the gzip exports (:1934-1964 — comment says 'hash every index file remotely'); on sync failure only pass_ok=False + one events line — bk['watermarks'] is saved unconditionally (:2155), so those rows are never re-exported/re-synced (next pass exports only newer rows; day_dirs derive from new_rels only). sync-pending.log is written ONLY when no remote is configured (:2093-2103), not when sync FAILS. Header claim ':104-110' ('(d) destination-side read-back verification ... rclone cat when remote') and dispatch F4(d) promise more than the code does. Server-loss today = silent permanent archive gap for any window whose sync failed once. Also: last_run_ts is set BEFORE the pass (:1971-1976), so a failed pass waits a full interval (default 24h) to retry.\n\n[F3] MAJOR — Cleanup-on-COMPLETED is not gated on deliverable/commit state: Observer automates the exact #460 lost-work class for DONE-marked builders with uncommitted work. act_completed (:942-979) runs kickoff_cleanup --yes (:949) BEFORE and independent of deliverable_check (:953); deliverable_present counts COMMITTED files only (merge-base diff, :914-931), so a builder that writes DONE with uncommitted work gets its worktree destroyed and the check then reports files_changed=0 over a corpse. Evidence bundle captures dirty status as text (:736-740) but preserves nothing. The design doc mandates cleanup-on-DONE (SC1), so this is spec-compliant — but nothing enforces the compensating discipline (commit-before-DONE) that makes it safe, and #460's 09:31 result comment documents this precise wipe as the project's worst recent loss. One-line reorder (gate cleanup on deliverable_present OR zero-dirty confirmation) closes it.\n\n[F4] MAJOR — One circuit-breaker pool gates comments AND lethal actions: an alert storm disarms the kill authority. breaker_allow (:473-488) is a single rolling window consumed by post_comment (:495), kickoff_cleanup (:830) and kickoff_stop (:847). Storm sources are plausible: doctrine validation posts per-dependent (:1589), fast-path UNKNOWN/PARKED alerts per unattributed session (:1714-1731), admission alerts (:2303). 24 comments/hour (default cap) → a genuinely frozen agent's stop/cleanup silently skipped that hour (kickoff_stop returns False without attempting; act_frozen still reports stop=FAILED). Safety throttle should not be able to starve the safety action it exists to bound.\n\n[F5] MAJOR — Crash between action execution and save_state loses the transition ledger while the actions persist → duplicate kills/cleanups/comments next cycle. State saves only at :2424; stop/cleanup/comment mutate the world earlier. Any uncaught exception mid-main() (e.g., max() over non-numeric time_updated at :2048, TypeError on tampered fastpath_seen values at :1690) → bash logs cycle-program-failed (:2480-2483) with state file untouched → next cycle re-reads pre-transition state and re-executes the frozen chain against an already-dead/cleaned agent (duplicate --force stops, duplicate comments). Idempotency rests entirely on volatile state that is written last.\n\n[F6] MAJOR — No single-instance guard despite the 'single writer per state dir' assumption (:447). No pidfile/lockfile exists anywhere. The documented deployment is a detached tmux loop (:158-161); a double-launch (or tmux resurrect alongside a live copy) yields two observers interleaving kills/cleanups/comments with last-writer-wins state — each believes the breaker state is its own. For a supervisor holding kill authority this needs an O_EXCL lockfile at startup.\n\n[F7] MAJOR — The R2 deliverable-check rebuild — the specific fix for the proven #460 false positive — has ZERO harness coverage. run-tests.sh contains no assertion on deliverable/merged/files_changed (grep count: 0); T1/T12 assert staging rows and phases only, with REPO_ROOT pointing at a nonexistent dir so every git path in deliverable_check short-circuits. The merge-base/diff/cat-file logic (:887-936) — including the cat-file -e branch:path existence loop (:926-931) — has never been executed by CI. (I verified manually that 'git cat-file -e rev:path --' is accepted by git, so the logic is plausible; but untested-is-untested.)\n\n===================== MINOR =====================\n\n[F8] MINOR — Failed/timed-out hot copies leave partial .db files that participate in generation pruning: timeout=600 kills sqlite3 mid-backup leaving dest (:1792-1794); prune_hot_generations sorts all *.db including partials (:1810-1824) — with KEEP=1 a corrupt newest generation evicts the last good one until the next successful pass self-heals.\n[F9] MINOR — OBSERVER_BACKUP_KEEP=0 deletes ALL generations including the snapshot just created (:1818 else-branch), then export opens the deleted file via immutable URI → store-green=False forever. Env foot-gun with no validation.\n[F10] MINOR — Incremental watermark uses strict > (:1846-1850, advance =max(tu) :2048): a row updated in the same millisecond as the watermark AFTER the snapshot is skipped permanently. Classic incremental-export loss window; ms collisions on a busy store are plausible.\n[F11] MINOR — DRY_RUN does not gate the backup pass: header claims dry-run executes no mutations (:154-156) but wave_backup performs real hot-copies/writes (:1967+) on the first cycle of any dry-run smoke test (last_run_ts=0). Builder patched the HARNESS (run-tests.sh:71-74) instead of the script.\n[F12] MINOR — OBSERVER_DRY_RUN=0 ENABLES dry-run: OB_DRY_RUN=\"${OBSERVER_DRY_RUN:+1}\" (:255) coerces any non-empty value (including '0') to 1. Inconsistent with FAST_PATH's direct ==\"1\" compare.\n[F13] MINOR — Catalog outage = 30-min fail-open: load_catalog caches ts even when ALL providers returned empty (:2186-2199), so model_valid returns None (undecidable) for the whole TTL; checks log ok:null and continue (:2238-2242) with no surfacing alert. Admission is audit-only anyway (see F14), so impact is muted visibility.\n[F14] MINOR — 'Pre-dispatch admission checks' never gate anything: dispatch already happened; checks are post-hoc audits over tracked agents (:2214-2287). MODEL_PATTERNS also over-extract prose (a KICKOFF mentioning a fallback model flags it invalid, :2172-2176). Claim-vs-code naming gap; noise-prone.\n[F15] MINOR — Unbounded growth + ephemeral evidence: terminal agents are never pruned from state['agents'] (:2339-2355 leaves cleaned/orphan-flagged forever); events.jsonl, evidence/, preserved-worktrees.txt, python-stderr.log all grow without rotation; and ALL of it — including sha256-manifested forensic bundles — lives in /tmp (:186, :297-301), so a reboot destroys the evidence trail the hashes anchor. Hashes are self-referential (computed by the same writer; no external anchoring).\n[F16] MINOR — Fast-path PARKED promise is conditional: header says tracked agents enter PARKED same cycle (:71-82), but attribution requires session_ids recorded by a prior deep probe (:1694-1697); never-probed agents produce repeated unattributed flag-issue alerts instead (:1713-1717).\n[F17] MINOR — findings_exist verification counts ANY bracketed timestamp incl. Observer's own earlier comments (:981-995) — FINISHED-UNMARKABLE verification can self-satisfy.\n[F18] MINOR — Doctrine reverse-edge lookup misses deletions (changed requires k in previous AND current, :1562-1563) and never-baselined new files; DOC_UNIVERSE is cached for process lifetime (:1431-1433) so basename resolution goes stale in long runs.\n[F19] MINOR — OOM ambiguity: organic SIGKILL (kernel OOM) is indistinguishable from orchestrator stop (exit 137, :401,:1045-1051) → KILLED path cleans the worktree (:1086-1093), destroying residue the FAILED path would preserve. Low frequency, but blast radius is the known worst loss class.\n[F20] MINOR — T16 is non-hermetic (proven in my run): issue-exists/issue-claimed exercise the LIVE crosslink CLI against #460 and require CWD inside a crosslink repo (run-tests.sh:483-488 acknowledges the live-issue part; the repo-context part surfaced only when I ran from /tmp → 96/98). Suite red/green depends on where and when it runs.\n[F21] MINOR — issue-claimed parses brittle human text ('is locked by', :2269-2271); wording drift → false violations. Backup pass also blocks the single-threaded supervision loop for minutes (600s/store timeout + integrity_check + sha256 + gzip, :1792+), delaying all monitoring incl. kill responses.\n\nCOMPLETENESS vs specs: R1-R4 ✓ (rename, deliverable rebuild [untested: F7], harness triage, incremental commits visible in history). F1 ✓ F2 ✓ F3 ✓ F4 ✓(with F2/F8-F10 gaps) F5 ✓(F13/F14 caveats) F6 ✓. Six-verdict matrix fully consumed; wave-anomaly scan-based tracking is correct; parked suppression/expiry structure matches design. Design-doc items outside dispatch scope (wind-down protocol, provenance-recorded contract check) absent — correctly out of scope per the dispatch spec's F1-F6 list, noted for the record.\n\nVERDICT RATIONALE: CONDITIONAL — land-and-operate is defensible for shakedown n=1 WITH: (1) F1 floor (resume_at = max(parsed, now+grace)) + rate_limited recheck before expiry-kill; (2) F2 fix (hold watermarks on sync failure + retry backlog + remote export read-back); (3) F3 reorder (deliverable-gated cleanup); (4) F4 reserved breaker budget for stop/cleanup; (5) F6 lockfile. F5/F7 should follow before the tool is trusted unsupervised.\n\nWHY: the brief asks whether each path does what we actually want, not what is written — the write-up claims (verified export, safe auto-kill, dry-run purity, single-writer) outrun the code in four places, and the two historical trauma classes (#460 lost worktree, misjudged frozen agents) map exactly onto F3 and F1.\nWHAT: evidence = full read of both files at 827204cb against the three named references; scanner matrix cross-check; bash -n clean; harness executed (96/98, both fails root-caused environmental, mechanism proven); targeted manual verifications (git cat-file -- acceptance, locks-check phrasing, crosslink CWD dependence).\nHOW CERTAIN: findings F1-F6, F8-F21 evidence-based (code lines cited, mechanically checked). F7 evidence-based (grep count 0 + harness REPO_ROOT tracing). F1's real-world trigger frequency guess-level (depends on opencode's actual retry-after log format, which I could not confirm from inputs).\nWHAT-NOT-TESTED: non-dry-run mutations end-to-end (orchestrator shakedown pending); rclone positive path (no remote wired); RED verification branch (no fault injection); long-duration loop behavior; concurrent-instance behavior (F6 asserted from code absence, not demonstrated); opencode.log retry-after formats in the wild (F1 misparse probability); behavior under state-file tampering.","created_at":"2026-08-25T03:51:40.185492079Z","kind":"result","signed_by":"SHA256:yJqhVs6VvSA+R/sRTHH+7fRyRM6vGnIAKr5wwzBuj0Q","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgbuqwMJup+uI/6mBaINp3WLN626geZ1OtlycfDKNmaL4AAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1lbnQAAAAAAAAABnNoYTUxMgAAAFMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAABAPonb8hUpsqQWuaVxi3uTZWhkSot4mAr+F2/MBvipNYEU2FdTHyLf9q7rWJOSc1HSAz5MJUnn1Zn67kx3YTjYCA=="},"signed_by":"SHA256:yJqhVs6VvSA+R/sRTHH+7fRyRM6vGnIAKr5wwzBuj0Q","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgbuqwMJup+uI/6mBaINp3WLN626geZ1OtlycfDKNmaL4AAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQFzf7jOfc2S4CKIq7XXoCQL5f+ul9rwoPhih9fboU/vkcPXORyVZX0OYJFmaKCFJn51+PebV+KHziuj3eqmldww="}
